 note = {Part Two of Analysis in Context focuses on one composition by Beethoven: his Grand Fugue, Op. 133, for string quartet, composed in 1825 and published posthumously in 1827. From its premiere performance, Op. 133, along with Op. 134 (which is Beethoven’s own version for four-hand piano), developed a reputation for being “incomprehensible.” This extreme case of “late Beethoven”—the crazy music of an old and deaf composer—is used to ask the following question: how has wanting to understand Beethoven—a critical endeavor that started well during Beethoven’s lifetime—changed the way we listen to, perform, or analyze music?},
